<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The fortnightly update check.

  A separate agent from the server's, deliberately. This one is allowed to fail,
  is allowed to be throttled, and must never be able to take the tracker down
  with it. The server agent is Interactive because she is waiting on it; this
  one is Background because nobody is.

  WHY WEEKLY FOR A FORTNIGHTLY CHECK
  StartCalendarInterval is a calendar matcher, not an interval: it can say
  "every Monday" or "the 1st and the 15th", and it cannot say "every 14 days".
  The two honest ways to build a fortnight out of it are:

    (a) fire on two days of the month, but the 1st and the 15th are 13, 14, 16
        or 17 days apart depending on the month, and February drifts;
    (b) fire weekly and let the server refuse a check it has already done.

  (b) is chosen. The floor already exists in the server: a GET of /api/update
  runs a check behind the answer only if one is due, against
  checkIntervalHours in the update source file. Set that to 336 for a
  fortnight. The floor therefore lives in exactly one place, next to the
  timestamp it is comparing against, so the schedule here can be as generous
  as it likes without ever meaning more than one check a fortnight. That is also
  what makes RunAtLoad safe below.

  WHY RunAtLoad AS WELL
  launchd re-fires a StartCalendarInterval it missed while the Mac was asleep,
  soon after it wakes. It does NOT reliably do the same for a Mac that was shut
  down at the appointed minute. RunAtLoad closes that hole: the agent is loaded
  at every login, so a Mac that spends Mondays switched off still checks the
  next time she uses it. With the server-side floor in place the extra fires
  cost one loopback request that returns immediately.

  WHY 12:20 ON A MONDAY
  A laptop is awake in the middle of a working day and asleep at 03:00, and a
  check that fires while the Mac is awake is a check that happens now instead of
  whenever it next wakes. The odd minute keeps it off the top of the hour, where
  everything else on the machine is scheduled.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
  It never installs anything, and it cannot: the only verb it uses is GET. The
  server looks for a new version behind that answer and records what it found;
  the button in Settings -> Updates is the only thing that applies one. Silent
  auto-install on a system of record means she finds out by the thing being
  different, which is the opposite of the promise.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>@@LABEL@@</string>

	<!--
	  $0 is "sh", $1 is common.sh. The script is passed as an argument rather
	  than built into a string, so nothing in a path needs escaping - the same
	  rule ibc_dialog follows for the same reason.

	  GET, not POST. GET is the interval-floored path: the server checks behind
	  the answer only when one is due. A POST of {"action":"check"} would force
	  a check on every fire, which would turn this weekly wake-up into a weekly
	  check. It also means this job holds no verb that can change anything.

	  The HTTP status is checked explicitly. curl exits 0 when it connected to
	  something and got any answer at all, including a 404 from a foreign server
	  squatting on the port, so its exit code is a claim and the status line is
	  the evidence. Same lesson the launcher learned from launchctl kickstart.
	-->
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/sh</string>
		<string>-c</string>
		<string>. "$1" || exit 1
IBC_LOG_TARGET="$IBC_LOG_DIR/update.log"
if ! PORT=$(ibc_running_port); then
  ibc_log "update check: no server is answering, skipped"
  exit 0
fi
CODE=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 300 "http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/api/update" 2>/dev/null)
case "$CODE" in
  '' | 000) CODE=000 ;;
esac
ibc_log "update check: GET /api/update on port $PORT answered $CODE"
if [ "$CODE" != "200" ]; then
  exit 1
fi
</string>
		<string>sh</string>
		<string>@@COMMON_SH@@</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Weekly. The fortnight is enforced by the server, which knows when it last
	  checked; see the note at the top of this file.
	-->
	<key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
	<dict>
		<key>Weekday</key>
		<integer>1</integer>
		<key>Hour</key>
		<integer>12</integer>
		<key>Minute</key>
		<integer>20</integer>
	</dict>

	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  No KeepAlive. A check that fails is a check that failed; relaunching it in
	  a loop would hammer GitHub and fill the log, and the next calendar fire is
	  a week away at most.
	-->
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>300</integer>

	<!--
	  Background here is correct, unlike the server agent, where it caused an
	  18.9s boot. Nobody is waiting on this job, so the disk-I/O throttling that
	  wrecked the server is exactly the right treatment for it.
	-->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Background</string>
	<key>LowPriorityIO</key>
	<true/>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>@@STDOUT@@</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>@@STDERR@@</string>

	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>HOME</key>
		<string>@@HOME@@</string>
		<!-- launchd hands a job almost no PATH, and this one needs curl. -->
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>@@PATH@@</string>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
